        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>AssaultRunner Pro Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The AssaultRunner Pro treadmill is a well-made, motorless curved trainer for people who
            want to run hard indoors — sprints, intervals, conditioning work — and it is a poor fit for
            walking, for steady easy miles, or for anyone who needs to fold a machine away.</strong> At
            about $3,499 it costs as much as a premium motorised treadmill with incline, so the question is
            less "is it good?" than "is a curved trainer the right tool for you?"
          </p>
          <p>
            Its core figures are consistent wherever we looked: a 17 by 62-inch slat running surface, a
            350 lb user rating, a 280 lb frame, no motor and no need for a power outlet. Two things are
            less settled. The frame warranty is 7 years on Assault's current page but 5 years on a US
            dealer listing and in independent reviews, and Assault's own store showed the Pro sold out on
            the day we checked, while a dealer listed it as available. Get the warranty term in writing
            from whoever sells it to you, and check stock before planning around a delivery date.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Pro is sold through Assault and its dealers, not on Amazon, so the card here shows the
            nearest Amazon alternative, the SOLE C80, a curved trainer of the same length with magnetic
            resistance. If you are deciding between curved trainers, our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best curved treadmills</a> roundup puts the Pro next to the SOLE C80, TrueForm and Woodway machines. If you are deciding
            whether you want a manual treadmill at all, start with <a href="/how-to-use-a-manual-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a manual treadmill</a>.
            We have not tested the Pro; this review is built from the published figures and from what
            independent reviewers consistently report.
          </p>
        </div>
