              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price shown</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not captured</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,499</td>
                <td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,499</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The Pro is the middle of the range and, for most home buyers, the sensible one. The Classic saves
            money at the cost of a shorter warranty and a smaller console. The Elite is worth considering
            mainly for a heavier user who wants the 400 lb rating, or a gym that wants the labour cover and
            the lifetime belt. For a single home runner within the Pro's 350 lb rating, the extra $1,000 buys
            margin and cover rather than a different running experience.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Alternatives Worth Comparing</h2>
<p>
            The Pro's nearest rivals, all covered with verified figures in our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a> and our <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual treadmill roundup</a>,
            differ from it in ways that matter more than their badges.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>SOLE C80.</strong> A curved slat trainer with a wider 19 by 62-inch surface and eight levels of magnetic resistance, rated to 330 lb and usually priced well below the Pro. The wider deck and the resistance are its two advantages; the Pro's longer track record and higher user rating are its own.</li>
            <li><strong>TrueForm Trainer.</strong> A shallower curve — <a href="/trueform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>TrueForm</a> says the shallowest on the market, and independent reviews describe it as shallower than rivals — with a 400 lb rating, at a similar or slightly higher price. Worth a look for runners who care about a natural stride more than sprint response.</li>
            <li><strong>Woodway Curve LTG.</strong> <a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Woodway</a>'s home-oriented curved trainer, rated to 350 lb, with a TPE slat belt Woodway describes as softer than average. It costs about the same or a little more, when you can get one.</li>
            <li><strong>A motorised treadmill with incline.</strong> For the same $3,500, or much less, a motorised machine gives you pace control, incline and often folding. If your training is mostly steady running and walking, that is the better buy. Our <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs motorised comparison</a> sets out where each wins.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Do not compare the Pro with $300 to $500 machines sold as "curved" <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>folding treadmills</a>; they are a
            different product, and our roundups explain why.
          </p>
          <p>
            A note on safety records: we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall listings
            on 14 October 2026 and found no recall or safety warning involving Assault Fitness or the
            AssaultRunner.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy the AssaultRunner Pro</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want to run hard indoors.</strong> Sprinters, field-sport and combat-sport
            athletes, and anyone whose training is built on intervals and conditioning will get the most from
            a belt with no speed ceiling that responds immediately to effort.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want a machine with no motor to fail and no outlet to find.</strong> For a
            garage gym or an outbuilding, a well-built motorless trainer is a long-lived piece of equipment,
            and the 150,000-mile belt term reflects that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are within its 350 lb rating and have a permanent place for it.</strong> It
            does not fold, and at 280 lb it stays where it is put.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look at the SOLE C80 if you want resistance or a wider surface</strong>, and at the
            TrueForm Trainer if a natural, shallow-curve stride matters more than sprint response.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy it to walk, or to run easy miles at a set pace.</strong> A motorised treadmill
            does those jobs better for the same money or less, with incline and pace control included.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whatever you decide, confirm two things before paying:</strong> that the seller has stock
            and a delivery date, and which frame warranty term applies to your unit, in writing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying an AssaultRunner Pro</h2>
          <p>Most disappointed buyers of curved trainers bought the right machine for someone else’s training. These are the avoidable errors.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying it mainly to walk</h3>
              <p>The Pro can be walked on, but it is a sprint and conditioning machine with a 17-inch surface. For mostly walking, a motorised treadmill with incline or a flat manual walker does the job as well for much less money.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting motorised-treadmill paces</h3>
              <p>You drive the belt yourself, so the same speed takes more effort; one independent review likens it to running uphill. Train to heart rate or effort when you move between machines, rather than chasing the pace you run on a powered belt.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not confirming the frame warranty</h3>
              <p>Assault’s current page says 7 years on the frame; a dealer listing and independent reviews say 5. Two years of frame cover is worth a short email. Ask the seller which term applies and keep the answer with your order.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Planning around a delivery date without confirmed stock</h3>
              <p>Assault’s store showed the Pro sold out on 13 and 14 October 2026, while a dealer listed it as available. Ask for a confirmed delivery window before paying, and be wary of prices well below $3,499 without a clear reason.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Buy the AssaultRunner Pro if you will run hard on it and can give a
            280 lb, non-folding machine a permanent home.</strong> Its 17 by 62-inch curved slat surface,
            350 lb rating, no-motor design and 150,000-mile belt cover are consistent across Assault and
            independent sources, and for sprints and conditioning it does what a motorised treadmill cannot.
          </p>
          <p>
            For walking or pace-set easy running, a motorised treadmill is the better use of $3,499. If a
            curved trainer is right for you, compare the Pro with the wider, resistance-equipped SOLE C80 in
            our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">curved treadmill roundup</a>,
            and confirm stock and the frame warranty term in writing before you pay.
          </p>
        </div>

